---
title: 'CI Configuration File'
description: 'Configure Nx Cloud CI and external CI providers with the .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml file: workers, launch templates, Ultracache, and self-healing.'
filter: 'type:References'
---

Nx Cloud reads CI configuration from a `.nx/pipelines/ci.yaml` file in your workspace.
The same file format works whether your pipeline runs on [Nx Cloud CI](/docs/features/ci-features#run-ci-on-nx-cloud) or on an external provider such as GitHub Actions.

{% aside type="note" title="Using .nx/ci-config.yaml?" %}
We've added support for `.nx/pipelines/ci.yaml`. See the [migration guide](/docs/kb/migrate-from-ci-config).
{% /aside %}

{% tabs syncKey="ci-location" %}
{% tabitem label="External CI" %}

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
worker-templates:
  default:
    workers: 5 linux-large-js
    stop-after:
      - build
      - test
```

Start the workers once, early in the main job of your provider's workflow:

```shell
npx nx-cloud start-workers
```

{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="Nx Cloud CI" %}

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
on:
  pull-requests:
    event: PR
  main-pushes:
    event: PUSH
    branches: [main]

worker-templates:
  default:
    workers: 5 linux-large-js
    stop-after:
      - build
      - test

jobs:
  main:
    runs-on: linux-medium-js
    workers: default
    steps:
      - run: npx nx affected -t lint test build
```

{% /tabitem %}
{% /tabs %}

## Top-level structure

| Section            | Purpose                                             |
| ------------------ | --------------------------------------------------- |
| `worker-templates` | Named worker setups: counts, sizes, and task rules. |
| `launch-templates` | Machine setups that workers and jobs run on.        |
| `ultracache`       | Paths that Ultracache ignores.                      |
| `self-healing`     | Which tasks self-healing CI can fix.                |
| `name`             | Display name of the pipeline.                       |
| `on`               | Events that start the jobs.                         |
| `env`              | Environment variables for every job and worker.     |
| `defaults`         | Defaults for the steps in every job.                |
| `jobs`             | The jobs and their steps.                           |

External CI doesn't run `name`, `on`, `env`, `defaults`, and `jobs`, but still validates them, so one file can serve both kinds of run in the same repository.

{% aside type="note" title="File name" %}
Nx Cloud CI runs every `.yaml` or `.yml` file in `.nx/pipelines` that has an `on` section, so you can name the file something other than `ci.yaml`.
{% /aside %}

For editor validation, add the published schema to the first line of the file:

```yaml
# yaml-language-server: $schema=https://cloud.nx.app/nx-cloud/static/schemas/workflow.schema.json
```

## `worker-templates`

Named worker setups, at most 50 entries.
On external CI, [`start-workers`](/docs/reference/nx-cloud-cli#nx-cloud-start-workers) starts one entry.
On Nx Cloud CI, a job picks one with `workers: <name>`.

An entry is a count string or a map:

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
worker-templates:
  # A count string
  nightly: 10 linux-large-js

  # A fixed count with task rules
  release:
    workers: 2 linux-medium-js; 1 linux-large-js
    stop-after: [build]

  # Sized by the work in each run
  pr:
    small: 2 linux-medium-js
    medium: 3 linux-medium-js; 1 linux-large-js
    large: 8 linux-medium-js; 4 linux-large-js
    fail-after: 1
```

A count string lists `<count> <launch-template>` pairs separated by `;`, with at most 20 templates.
The template names can come from your [`launch-templates`](#launch-templates) or from the [pre-built templates](/docs/kb/launch-templates).

External CI has no job to take a setup from, so every count needs a template name.
A bare count such as `3` only works on Nx Cloud CI, where it reuses the machine setup of the job.

### `workers`

A fixed count of workers, all started with the run.
Can't be combined with `small`, `medium`, and `large`.

### `small`, `medium`, and `large`

Worker counts sized by the work the run has to do.
All three are required.

- `small` starts with the run, every time.
- At each distributed command, Nx Cloud compares the remaining work, after cache hits, with the stored timings of your workspace and picks a size.
- Counts are totals per template, so picking a larger size adds the difference. A template's count can't go down from one size to the next, and workers are never removed.

By default, `small` covers up to 33% of the stored task time, `medium` up to 66%, and `large` the rest.
`small` and `medium` can set their own upper limit, a whole percent from 1% to 99%.
`small`'s limit must be below `medium`'s, and a size without `workload` keeps its default:

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
worker-templates:
  pr:
    small: { count: 2 linux-medium-js, workload: 10% }
    medium: 4 linux-medium-js
    large: 6 linux-medium-js
```

### `assignment-rules`

Rules that send matching tasks to specific launch templates.
For the rule format, see [assignment rules](/docs/kb/assignment-rules).

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
worker-templates:
  default:
    workers: 3 linux-medium-js; 1 linux-large-js
    assignment-rules:
      - targets: [e2e-ci*]
        run-on:
          - agent: linux-large-js
            parallelism: 1
```

### `scheduling-constraints`

Tasks that must never run on the same worker at the same time, for example because they share a port or a database.
Each entry selects tasks by `projects`, `targets`, or `configurations` globs, and `cannot-run-with` lists the tasks they can't overlap with on one worker.

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
worker-templates:
  default:
    workers: 3 linux-large-js
    scheduling-constraints:
      # e2e tasks share a resource, so never run two of them on one worker at once
      - targets: ['e2e*']
        cannot-run-with:
          - targets: ['e2e*']
```

Tasks outside the constraint still share the worker with them.
To give a task a worker to itself, set `parallelism: false` on its target in [project configuration](/docs/reference/project-configuration).

### `stop-after`

Targets after which workers shut down: once a command running each listed target has started, workers stop when they have no tasks left.
Include at least one target from each Nx command your pipeline runs.

Type: array of strings.

### `fail-after`

Number of task failures after which the run is marked failed.
`0` means never fail, so the run always finishes. `1` stops on the first failure.

Type: `integer`, minimum `0`. Default: `1`.

### `main-job-worker`

Runs distributed tasks on a background worker on the machine of the main job, using the CPU and memory you allow it.
External CI doesn't start this worker from `ci.yaml`.

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
worker-templates:
  default:
    workers: 4 linux-large-js
    main-job-worker:
      cpu: 3
      memory: 6GB
```

## `launch-templates`

Machine setups, keyed by the name that count strings and `runs-on` refer to.
For the template format, see [launch templates](/docs/kb/launch-templates).

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
launch-templates:
  linux-custom:
    resource-class: docker_linux_amd64/medium
    image: ubuntu22.04-node24.14-v1
    init-steps:
      - name: Checkout
        uses: nrwl/nx-cloud-workflows/v6/workflow-steps/checkout/main.yaml
      - name: Install node modules
        uses: nrwl/nx-cloud-workflows/v6/workflow-steps/install-node-modules/main.yaml
```

Nx Cloud looks up a template name in `ci.yaml` and the files it imports, then in `.nx/workflows`, then in the public `nrwl/nx-cloud-workflows` templates.

## `ultracache`

Paths that [Ultracache](/docs/features/ci-features/remote-cache#ultracache) ignores when it records what a task reads and writes.

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
ultracache:
  ignore-reads: ['dist/target/**']
  ignore-writes: ['dist/target/**']
  task-exclusions:
    - project: app-e2e
      ignore-reads: ['apps/app/build/**']
```

Entries in `task-exclusions` select tasks by `project`, `target`, and `configuration`.
For when to use each option, see [Ultracache sandboxing](/docs/kb/sandboxing).

## `self-healing`

Overrides for [self-healing CI](/docs/features/ci-features/self-healing-ci#advanced-configuration-overrides).
On external CI, these apply to runs started with `nx-cloud start-workers`.

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
self-healing:
  fix-tasks-patterns: ['*lint*', '*test*', '!*e2e*']
  auto-apply-patterns: ['lint*']
```

### `fix-tasks-patterns`

Glob patterns of task IDs that self-healing CI is allowed to attempt fixes for.

### `auto-apply-patterns`

Glob patterns of task IDs whose fixes are applied automatically without explicit approval.

## Import a section from another file

`launch-templates`, `ultracache`, and `worker-templates` can each be a path to another file.

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
launch-templates: .nx/pipelines/launch-templates.yaml
ultracache: .nx/pipelines/ultracache.yaml
worker-templates: .nx/pipelines/workers.yaml
```

Paths are relative to the repository root and read at the commit of the run.
The imported file holds only the content of the section, with no top-level key naming it, and can't import again.
A `launch-templates` file can also keep a top-level `launch-templates:` key, so an existing `.nx/workflows/agents.yaml` can be imported as it is.

## `on`

Named triggers that start the jobs.
Each trigger needs an `event` of `PR` or `PUSH`.

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
on:
  pull-requests:
    event: PR
    branches: [main]
    paths-ignore: ['docs/**']
  main-pushes:
    event: PUSH
    branches: [main]
```

Triggers can also filter with `branches-ignore` and `paths`, `PUSH` triggers with `tags` and `tags-ignore`, and `PR` triggers with `types`.
A positive filter can't be combined with its `-ignore` counterpart.

## `jobs`

The jobs of the pipeline, each on its own machine.

| Key               | Description                                                                         |
| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `runs-on`         | Launch template the job runs on.                                                    |
| `resource-class`  | Machine size. Use with `image` and `init-steps` instead of `runs-on`.               |
| `image`           | Container image. Use with `resource-class` and `init-steps` instead of `runs-on`.   |
| `init-steps`      | Steps run once when the machine starts, such as checkout and install.               |
| `steps`           | Steps the job runs in order.                                                        |
| `workers`         | Workers that run the tasks of the job. Without it, the job uses its one machine.    |
| `needs`           | Job or jobs that must finish before this job starts.                                |
| `if`              | Condition that must hold for the job to run.                                        |
| `env`             | Environment variables for every step of the job.                                    |
| `defaults`        | Defaults for the steps of the job, such as `run.working-directory`.                 |
| `timeout-minutes` | Minutes before the job is stopped, at most 60.                                      |
| `permissions`     | Permissions of the repository token. Set `repository-contents: write` to push tags. |
| `name`            | Display name of the job.                                                            |

### `jobs.<job>.workers`

| Written as                                   | Means                                                                  |
| -------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `workers: 3`                                 | Three workers with the machine setup of the job, started with the job. |
| `workers: 10 linux-large-js`                 | A fixed count of a launch template, started with the job.              |
| `workers: { small: 3, medium: 6, large: 8 }` | [Sized by the work](#small-medium-and-large).                          |
| `workers: <name>`                            | An entry from `worker-templates`.                                      |
| No `workers`                                 | One machine, no workers.                                               |

Workers get the `env` of the file and the job.

### `jobs.<job>.steps`

A step runs a shell command with `run`, or a step template with `uses` and `with`.
It can also set `name`, `id`, `if`, `env`, `shell`, `working-directory`, `continue-on-error`, and `timeout-minutes`.

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
jobs:
  main:
    runs-on: linux-medium-js
    steps:
      - name: Validate dependencies
        if: nxcloud.event_name == 'pull_request'
        run: node ./tools/validate-deps.mjs
      - name: Affected tasks
        run: npx nx affected -t lint test build
```

### Coming from GitHub Actions

| GitHub Actions                                                   | Nx Cloud CI                                          |
| ---------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| `on: push` and `on: pull_request`                                | Named triggers with `event: PUSH` or `event: PR`     |
| `runs-on: ubuntu-latest`                                         | A [launch template](/docs/kb/launch-templates)       |
| `actions/checkout`, `actions/setup-node`, `npm ci`               | `init-steps` of the launch template                  |
| `nrwl/nx-set-shas`                                               | Automatic                                            |
| `start-workers`                                                  | `workers` on the job                                 |
| `nx fix-ci`                                                      | Automatic                                            |
| `uses: owner/repo@ref`                                           | `uses` with a [step template](/docs/kb/custom-steps) |
| `needs`, `if`, `env`, `timeout-minutes`                          | Same keys                                            |
| `${{ secrets.X }}`, `${{ vars.X }}`, `${{ needs.<job>.result }}` | Same expressions                                     |
| `GITHUB_ENV`, `GITHUB_OUTPUT`, `GITHUB_PATH`                     | Same files                                           |
| `github.event_name` in a condition                               | `nxcloud.event_name`                                 |

### Current limits

Nx Cloud CI doesn't support these yet:

- Pull requests from forks.
- Events other than push and pull request, including `schedule`, `workflow_dispatch`, and merge queues.
- `strategy.matrix`, `concurrency`, `services`, and job `outputs`.
- Actions from the GitHub Marketplace and `action.yml` files, including `actions/cache` and `actions/upload-artifact`. `uses` runs Nx Cloud step templates.
- OIDC identity tokens for cloud providers. Use secrets for now.
- macOS and Windows. Jobs run on Linux.

A job runs for at most 60 minutes, and that is also the default for `timeout-minutes`.
You can't turn off GitHub status checks while Nx Cloud CI is on.
Configuration errors in `ci.yaml` show on the GitHub check.

## How `start-workers` picks a file and an entry

On external CI, [`nx-cloud start-workers`](/docs/reference/nx-cloud-cli#nx-cloud-start-workers) reads `.nx/pipelines/ci.yaml`, or the file named by `--config`.
While `.nx/ci-config.yaml` exists, `start-workers` without `--workers` or `--config` keeps reading it, even if `ci.yaml` is there too.
With neither file, the command fails.

| `--workers` | Entries in `worker-templates` | Result                              |
| ----------- | ----------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| Given       | Includes the name             | Starts that entry.                  |
| Given       | Doesn't include the name      | An error that lists the entries.    |
| Not given   | Exactly one                   | Starts that entry.                  |
| Not given   | Several                       | An error that asks for `--workers`. |
| Not given   | None                          | An error.                           |

Nx Cloud reads the file from your repository at the commit of the run, so commit changes before they can take effect.
An invalid file fails the command with an error naming the file, the import, and the key.
